/*
========================================================================================
    Process-specific Resource Configuration
========================================================================================
    Resource allocation for each process.
    Error strategy and maxRetries are inherited from conf/base.config.
========================================================================================
*/

// The StarDist flag vocabulary for SEGMENT's stardist backend (bin/segment.py):
// load a model, normalize the nuclear channel by percentile, tile, detect, expand.
// It is INLINED into the `withName: 'SEGMENT'` ext.args closure below, at its one
// call site, and it has to stay there.
//
// Do NOT "helpfully" re-extract it into a `def starDistCommonFlags(params)` here.
// Nextflow 26's strict (v2) parser rejects a function declaration in a config file
// outright -- `Unexpected input: '('`, pointing at the open paren -- and config
// parsing aborts before a single process is instantiated. Both obvious escapes are
// dead ends too: a top-level `starDistCommonFlags = { ... }` binding is rejected with
// `Dynamic config options only allowed in process scope`, and a lib/*.groovy class
// method is invisible from here -- conf/*.config is parsed by a separate Nextflow
// ConfigParser pass, so a `StarDistArgs.commonFlags(params)` version does NOT fail to
// compile (config parsing is dynamic); it silently resolves the unrecognised class
// name against the enclosing ConfigObject and fails only when the ext.args closure
// actually runs, with the deeply misleading `No signature of method:
// groovy.util.ConfigObject.commonFlags()`. Full inlining at the call site is the only
// legal form. tests/test_nextflow_strict_syntax.py holds that line.
//
// Inlining costs nothing here because there IS only one call site. The list was
// factored out when SEG_QC_GEOJSON also ran StarDist and had rebuilt an equivalent
// list BY HAND in its own script: block -- two lists that had already drifted (the QC
// one passed no simplification tolerance and no configured expansion distance, so it
// silently used bin/segment_to_geojson.py's Python defaults). The reg_qc=2 QC now
// segments through SEGMENT itself (aliased SEG_QC_SEGMENT, see
// subworkflows/local/seg_qc.nf), so there is no second call site to keep in step and
// no way for one to drift: whichever backend params.seg_method selects, both the
// shipped segmentation and the QC's get that backend's flags from the one ext.args
// closure below.
//
// --use-gpu is NOT part of the list: it is shared by all three backends, not
// stardist-specific, so the ext.args closure appends it once for whichever backend ran.

/*
================================================================================
    STARE (tiled) per-task memory, derived from the parameter that sets it
================================================================================
    Every tiled process was designed so its peak memory is a function of a PARAMETER rather
    than of the slide -- see the header of subworkflows/local/adapters/tiled_adapter.nf. That
    property does hold: peak RSS is set by the window parameter and not by the slide, so it is
    the same on an 8192^2 slide and on a larger one, because each task reads a bounded window
    through lazy zarr regions.

    THE MAGNITUDE IS NOT UNIFORM, AND THIS HEADER USED TO CLAIM IT WAS. It said "measured peak
    RSS is 1.3-2.0 GB per task", full stop. That band is the PER-TILE tasks -- TILED_REG_TILE
    and TILED_STITCH, whose measurements are in the table below -- and it has never covered
    TILED_COARSE since the coarse anchor became a U-Net (DISK) rather than a cheap feature
    detector. COARSE's peak is linear in thumbnail AREA, `GB ~= 1.1 + 7.3 * Mpx`, so at the
    shipped `reg_tiled_mode = 'high'` (reg_tiled_coarse_max_dim 2048, 4.19 Mpx) it is ~31.7 GB
    against the 48 GB its own closure requests -- an order of magnitude outside the band, and
    the exact number the closure below is derived from. The parameter-not-slide property is
    unchanged; only the constant is. A reader sizing a queue off "1.3-2.0 GB per tiled task"
    would under-provision COARSE by ~24x.

    But the RESERVATIONS were flat constants, so the guarantee was only ever a comment. The
    need scales with `reg_tiled_tile` / `reg_tiled_halo` / `reg_tiled_out_tile`; the request
    did not. Raising a tile size therefore overran a hardcoded 8 GB and surfaced as a SIGKILL,
    not as a validation error -- and `--stub` cannot catch it, since these closures are only
    exercised by a real submission. Deriving the request from the same parameter closes that
    gap in the direction the design already claims.

    Measured peak RSS on this codebase (uint16 slides, one channel read per task):
      TILED_REG_TILE, window = tile + 2*halo:  1024^2 -> 463 MB   2560^2 -> 1331 MB   4608^2 -> 2492 MB
      TILED_STITCH,   window = out_tile:        512^2 -> 655 MB   1024^2 -> 1415 MB   4096^2 -> 3387 MB
    Both are linear in window megapixels over a fixed interpreter/numpy/scipy/skimage baseline
    (~0.3-0.6 GB), because every intermediate in warp_image / residual_displacement is one
    array the size of the window:
      REG_TILE ~= 0.4 GB + 0.10 GB/Mpx        STITCH ~= 0.6 GB + 0.17 GB/Mpx

    The request is that line DOUBLED with a 4 GB floor, so the shipped defaults reserve 4 GB
    (vs the 8 GB they asked for and the ~1.4 GB they use) while `--reg_tiled_tile 8192` now
    reserves ~16 GB instead of being killed at 8.

    Both formulas are INLINED into their process' own `memory = { ... }` closure below. The
    two are near-identical arithmetic over different constants, and that duplication is
    FORCED, not a style choice. They were plain top-level `def`s here until Nextflow 26's
    strict (v2) parser made a function declaration in a config file a hard `Unexpected input:
    '('`. Nothing else is legal either: a top-level `tiledWindowGb = { ... }` binding is
    rejected with `Dynamic config options only allowed in process scope`, and a lib/*.groovy
    class is not visible from conf/*.config at all -- the name resolves silently against
    ConfigObject and fails only when the closure runs. Even under the OLD parser the shared-
    helper refactor did not work: a top-level def could be CALLED from a closure, but inside
    that invocation it could not call another top-level def -- the body resolves against the
    closure's delegate, not the config script, and Nextflow aborted the task with `Unknown
    method invocation 'tiledWindowGb' on _parse_closure5 type`, at task submission, config
    parsing having accepted it happily. So each closure below stays self-contained. Verify any
    change here with a REAL submission (`-with-trace`, then read the memory column); `-stub`
    alone will not exercise it.
*/

process {

    /*
    ================================================================================
        Default Publishing Settings
    ================================================================================
    */

    // Every process declares its own publishDir in a withName: block below. This default
    // exists only to catch one that does not.
    //
    // It used to be a real path — `<outdir>/<process-name-lowercased>` — which meant a
    // process with no explicit block published to a run-level, patient-unaware directory
    // and nobody noticed. SPLIT_CHANNELS did exactly that: it names outputs by channel
    // ('PANCK.tiff'), so two patients sharing a marker overwrote each other in
    // <outdir>/split_channels/ on every multi-patient run, green.
    //
    // The path below is deliberately conspicuous rather than an error: a `publishDir`
    // closure that throws aborts the task, and conf/modules.config's errorStrategy has an
    // 'ignore' branch that would swallow it. A directory named
    // `_UNROUTED_PUBLISH/<process>` is inert, harmless, and impossible to mistake for
    // intended output — and tests/test_layout.py fails the build when a process reaches it.
    publishDir = [
        path: { "${params.outdir}/_UNROUTED_PUBLISH/${task.process.tokenize(':')[-1].toLowerCase()}" },
        mode: 'copy',
        saveAs: { filename -> filename.equals('versions.yml') ? null : filename }
    ]

    /*
    ================================================================================
        Resource Labels (used by processes)
    ================================================================================
    */

    withLabel: 'process_single' {
        cpus   = { 1                   }
        memory = { 12.GB * task.attempt }
        time   = { 8.h  * task.attempt }
    }

    withLabel: 'process_low' {
        cpus   = { 2                     }
        memory = { 32.GB * task.attempt  }
        time   = { 2.h   * task.attempt  }
    }

    withLabel: 'process_medium' {
        cpus   = { 4                               }
        memory = { 100.GB + 100.GB * task.attempt  }
        time   = { 4.h   * task.attempt            }
    }

    withLabel: 'process_high' {
        cpus   = { 8                               }
        memory = { 200.GB + 100.GB * task.attempt  }
        time   = { 12.h  * task.attempt            }
    }

    /*
    ================================================================================
        Pipeline Info
    ================================================================================
    */

    /*
    ================================================================================
        QC is non-gating for SCHEDULING, but a broken QC step still fails the run
    ================================================================================
        QC outputs are aggregated downstream purely via collect()/collectFile()/
        combine()/join(), none of which require a fixed item count — so a missing QC
        output is simply absent and never DEADLOCKS the DAG. That is the whole of
        what "non-gating" ever meant, and it is a statement about the graph, not
        about whether the run is allowed to report success without them.

        The terminal branch used to be 'ignore', which conflated the two. It threw
        away an ordinary exit 1 — a Python traceback — on attempt 1, and this
        selector covers WARP_SEG_QC while reg_qc=2 is the DEFAULT. Verified
        2026-08-25: `beforeScript = 'exit 1'` on WARP_SEG_QC produced SHELL EXIT 0,
        a full-looking output tree, and ZERO *_seg_qc.json — no residual CSVs, and
        a SpatialData .zarr missing its obsm blocks. A green run must imply a
        complete output tree; that is what 'finish' below buys.

        'finish' rather than 'terminate' so in-flight tasks drain and the run
        reports every failure at once rather than only the first.

        maxRetries is pinned here (not inherited) so the closure's retry cutoff stays
        in lockstep with the budget on every profile — a dynamic strategy that returns
        'retry' after the budget is exhausted is treated as fatal, which is exactly
        what we must avoid. This withName selector overrides the global process-level
        errorStrategy/maxRetries set in base.config and the test configs.
    */
    withName: 'GENERATE_PREPROCESS_QC|GENERATE_REGISTRATION_QC|GENERATE_POSTPROCESSING_QC|GENERATE_QC_REPORT|SEG_QC_SEGMENT|SEG_QC_GEOJSON|WARP_SEG_QC' {
        maxRetries    = 3
        // POLICY: retry-then-fail. The named set lives in
        // tests/test_error_strategy_policy.py, which pins this text verbatim —
        // conf/*.config cannot see lib/ and NF26's parser forbids sharing a helper
        // between withName: blocks, so naming is all the abstraction available.
        //
        // 130..145 is the whole "killed by a signal" range (nf-core's convention), not a
        // hand-picked subset. The observed WARP_SEG_QC kill was 140 = 128+12 = SIGUSR2 —
        // SLURM's pre-walltime warning, NOT the SIGTERM the old comment claimed. Anything
        // outside the enumerated set used to be ignored on attempt 1, so a SIGXCPU (152 is
        // out of range, but 138/141/142 are not) threw away the whole retry budget.
        errorStrategy = { task.exitStatus in ((130..145) + 104) && task.attempt <= 3 ? 'retry' : 'finish' }
    }

    /*
    ================================================================================
        Pipeline Processes
    ================================================================================
    */

    withName: 'PREFLIGHT_SCALE' {
        // Resources come from its label ('process_single' in the module) -- this block
        // sets publishDir only, so it is not a second resource owner (see the one-owner
        // rule and tests/test_resource_label_coverage.py).
        publishDir = [
            path: { "${params.outdir}/qc" },
            mode: 'copy',
            pattern: "preflight_scale_report.json"
        ]
    }

    withName: 'CONVERT_IMAGE' {
        cpus   = 1
        memory = {
            def file_gb = (image_file.size() >> 30) ?: 1
            (file_gb < 4 ? 0.GB : file_gb < 12 ? 24.GB : file_gb < 24 ? 48.GB : 64.GB) + 24.GB * task.attempt
        }
        time   = { 2.h  * task.attempt }
        ext.args = ''
        publishDir = [
            path: { "${params.outdir}/${meta.patient_id}/converted" },
            // INTERMEDIATE. Not published at all at the default --cleanup_level=final,
            // rather than published and then deleted: publishDir is mode: 'copy', so the
            // delete variant pays a full copy out of work/ for a file nobody reads.
            // Gating the publish itself costs nothing.
            //
            // Pass --cleanup_level=none when this run's output will be RE-ENTERED --
            // `--start <step>` reads it, and mode='add_cycle' reads registered/ and the
            // segmentation masks (ParamUtils.validateCleanup refuses add_cycle at any
            // other level, at launch).
            //
            // The literal is INLINED at every site rather than shared: conf/*.config
            // cannot see lib/*.groovy, and NF26's parser forbids a helper shared between
            // withName: blocks. Layout.FINAL_KINDS owns the decision;
            // tests/test_cleanup_publish_gates.py is what keeps these copies agreeing
            // with it.
            //
            // The gate lives in `saveAs:`, a closure Nextflow resolves PER FILE at
            // publish time with the FINAL merged params. It must not be `enabled:` in
            // either form:
            //   * `enabled: { ... }` -- the closure OBJECT is coerced (to false), so every
            //     gated publish is dropped at every level (measured 2026-08-25).
            //   * `enabled: params.cleanup_level == 'none'` -- a plain boolean, evaluated
            //     EAGERLY at this line, which nextflow.config reaches BEFORE `profiles {}`
            //     and before any `-c` file is merged. It froze against the shipped
            //     'final', so a profile pin or the README's `-c site.config` route was
            //     silently ignored: 32 published files either way, 64 only via the CLI
            //     flag (measured 2026-09-06 on main @ c201f443).
            // Returning null skips the file; returning `fn` keeps its relative path, so
            // the published layout is byte-for-byte what `enabled: true` produced.
            // Guarded statically by tests/test_cleanup_publish_gates.py and
            // behaviourally by tests/cleanup_level_pin.sh (profile, -c none, -c final).
            saveAs: { fn -> params.cleanup_level == 'none' ? fn : null },
            mode: 'copy',
            pattern: "*.ome.tif",
            overwrite: true
        ]
    }

    withName: 'TILE_FOR_BASIC' {
        cpus   = 2
        // Streams: the grid is laid out by pure arithmetic (fov_tiling.fov_positions) and
        // each pseudo-FOV is read as its own region and written straight out, so the tile
        // buffer is O(preproc_tile_size^2) -- independent of both slide size and channel
        // count. See bin/tile_for_basic.py.
        //
        // NO FILE-SIZE TERM: CONVERT_IMAGE now writes its OME-TIFF tiled (bab9507,
        // guarded by tests/test_convert_streaming_write.py::test_the_write_is_tiled), so
        // tifffile's zarr view reports real chunk shapes and a region read decodes a TILE,
        // not a whole plane. Before that, a striped producer's chunks=(1, H, W) made a
        // region read decode the WHOLE PLANE and slice it -- measured, a 2048^2 region of
        // a 6000^2 striped plane peaked at 76.7 MiB against 16.0 MiB for the same read on
        // a tiled one. That is why an untiled producer ahead of ANY lazy reader in this
        // repo (this process, APPLY_PROFILES, TILED_STITCH) is a bug, not a style choice --
        // the property the tiling tests above guard, and SPLIT_CHANNELS' equivalent
        // (tests/test_split_multichannel_lazy_read.py::test_the_write_is_tiled).
        //
        // KNOWN DEGRADATION, NOT SIZED FOR HERE: a run resumed from a --prior_outdir
        // published before this branch, or an add_cycle run reading an older published
        // tree, can still hand this process an untiled slide, and the plane-decode cost
        // above returns for that input. That is covered by the exit-137 retry ramp
        // (conf/base.config), not by a term in this formula -- sizing the steady state for
        // an input the pipeline will stop producing would keep every normal run's request
        // permanently inflated.
        memory = {
            // Inlined at the call site: a shared helper is not expressible in a config
            // file under the strict parser (see the note above TILED_REG_TILE).
            def tile    = (params.preproc_tile_size as long)
            def buffers = 4d * tile * tile * 8d / (1024d * 1024d * 1024d)
            (Math.ceil(Math.max(6d, buffers)) as int).GB * task.attempt
        }
        time   = { 2.h * task.attempt }
        ext.args = ''
        // The tile stack and its sidecar are intermediates consumed by BASICPY and
        // APPLY_PROFILES. Publishing them would add a new Layout kind for artifacts
        // nothing downstream of this run reads.
        publishDir = [ enabled: false ]
    }

    /*
        BASICPY is modules/nf-core/basicpy, vendored unmodified. See that directory's
        MIRAGE-NOTES.md for the two ways it differs from every modules/local process
        (it errors under -profile conda by design, and its version emit is a hardcoded
        literal on a topic channel that mirage's versions.yml aggregation cannot collect).

        ============================================================================
        `ext.args` IS EMPTY ON PURPOSE. THIS IS A DECISION, NOT AN OVERSIGHT.
        ============================================================================
        The ruling is to run nf-core's BASICPY at UPSTREAM DEFAULTS, and not to
        reproduce mirage's previous in-process call. Do not "restore" the old
        parameters. What upstream's defaults are, and how they differ from what mirage
        used to do (`BaSiC(get_darkfield=True, smoothness_flatfield=1)`):

          flag / setting             mcmicro CLI default   mirage's OLD in-process call
          smoothness_flatfield             2.5                  1
          smoothness_darkfield             5.0                  1.0  (basicpy default)
          sparse_cost_darkfield            0.01                 0.01 (same)
          max_reweight_iterations          20                   10   (basicpy default)
          fitting_mode                     ladmap               ladmap (same)
          get_darkfield                    FALSE                TRUE
          sort_intensity                   False                False (same)
          device                           cpu                  cpu   (same)
          autotune                         OFF                  OFF   (same)

        THE CONSEQUENCE THAT IS NOT A TUNING DIFFERENCE: `get_darkfield` is False, so
        NO DARKFIELD IS ESTIMATED. Only the multiplicative flatfield is divided out; the
        additive offset mirage used to remove stays in the data. The module still emits a
        `*-dfp.ome.tif` -- basicpy leaves `darkfield` at its zero initialisation and
        resizes it to the tile shape, so the file is present, correctly shaped, and all
        zeros. bin/apply_basic_profiles.py detects that and says so in its log rather
        than pretending a darkfield was subtracted.

        AUTOTUNE IS OFF AT DEFAULT, AND THE FLAG THAT CONTROLS IT IS INVERTED.
        `--no_autotune` is declared `action="store_false"` with `default=True` and gated
        by `if not args.no_autotune:` -- so NOT passing it SKIPS autotune and PASSING it
        ENABLES autotune. Passing nothing is what gives the intended OFF. Do not "fix"
        the flag's name by adding it.

        tests/test_basicpy_defaults_are_deliberate.py fails if either `--darkfield` or
        `--no_autotune` reappears here, so this decision cannot be undone silently.
    */
    withName: 'BASICPY' {
        // Partial override: `label 'process_single'` in the vendored module still owns
        // cpus and time. Only memory is raised -- the module runs Bio-Formats under a JVM
        // and materialises one channel's tile stack for the fit, which does not fit the
        // single tier's 12 GB on a real slide.
        memory = { 32.GB * task.attempt }
        // Empty on purpose: upstream defaults, unmodified. See the block comment above.
        ext.args = ''
        // DIGEST-PINNED (ruling R6). Overridden here rather than edited into the vendored
        // module.nf, which stays byte-for-byte upstream (see modules/nf-core/basicpy/
        // MIRAGE-NOTES.md and tests/test_basicpy_module_is_vendored_unmodified.py). The
        // module's own line pins docker.io/labsyspharm/basicpy-docker-mcmicro:1.2.0-patch5;
        // this is that SAME image, digest only and no tag (Apptainer/Singularity documents
        // an image reference as NAME[:TAG|@DIGEST] and has known bugs with the combined
        // form, and the cluster pulls through Singularity), resolved 2026-09-02. Guarded by
        // tests/test_base_images_are_digest_pinned.py.
        container = 'docker.io/labsyspharm/basicpy-docker-mcmicro@sha256:355b14e2ec80b7b152272f333afd47234f007d0d37633b3ec948e87ec2c8e9b4'
        publishDir = [ enabled: false ]
    }

    withName: 'APPLY_PROFILES' {
        cpus   = 2
        // Streams one WRITE_TILE (2048px) output tile at a time and never assembles the
        // slide. It used to accumulate every corrected channel into a list, np.stack it,
        // then clip and cast the stack with the list still referenced -- ~8 x C x H x W
        // bytes, about 115 GB on an 8-channel 40000x30000 uint16 slide. That is what the
        // old `* 7.GB` was chasing, and it under-shot: the coefficient was applied to the
        // COMPRESSED file size. See bin/apply_basic_profiles.py -- INCLUDING its
        // `maxworkers=1` pin on the write itself: the output is generator-fed and
        // zlib-compressed, so without that pin tifffile's compressor pool queues encoded
        // tiles ahead of the writer, an unbounded term that scales with the channel count
        // at the container's pinned tifffile version (2025.5.10). This closure predates
        // that pin and was calibrated on a 2023.4.12 host, same as MERGE_AND_PYRAMID's.
        //
        // Two terms now, neither of them the assembled slide:
        //  * the PROFILE planes. BASICPY returns one per fitted channel at full pseudo-FOV
        //    resolution, held as float64 for flatfield and darkfield both:
        //    2 * C * preproc_tile_size^2 * 8 bytes. C is not visible in a config closure,
        //    so it is allowed for at 16 channels; a wider panel is absorbed by the retry ramp.
        //  * the write-tile buffers, O(2048^2).
        //
        // NO FILE-SIZE TERM: this process reads through the same lazy region path as
        // TILE_FOR_BASIC above -- see that block's comment for the untiled-producer
        // mechanism, the tests that now guard it, and the known --prior_outdir / add_cycle
        // degradation this closure deliberately does not size for.
        memory = {
            def tile     = (params.preproc_tile_size as long)
            def profiles = 2d * 16d * tile * tile * 8d / (1024d * 1024d * 1024d)
            def buffers  = 4d * 2048d * 2048d * 8d / (1024d * 1024d * 1024d)
            (Math.ceil(Math.max(8d, profiles + buffers)) as int).GB * task.attempt
        }
        time   = { 3.h * task.attempt }
        ext.args = ''
        // The same artifact the removed in-process PREPROCESS published, into the same
        // directory, so the `preprocessed` checkpoint row is unchanged by the backend swap.
        publishDir = [
            path: { "${params.outdir}/${meta.patient_id}/preprocessed" },
            // Intermediate — see tests/test_cleanup_publish_gates.py.
            saveAs: { fn -> params.cleanup_level == 'none' ? fn : null },
            mode: 'copy',
            pattern: "*_corrected.ome.tif",
            overwrite: true
        ]
    }

    withName: 'GENERATE_PREPROCESS_QC' {
        publishDir = [
            path: { "${params.outdir}/${meta.patient_id}/qc/preprocess" },
            mode: 'copy',
            pattern: "qc/*.png"
        ]
    }

    withName: 'GENERATE_POSTPROCESSING_QC' {
        publishDir = [
            path: { "${params.outdir}/${meta.patient_id}/qc/postprocessing" },
            mode: 'copy',
            pattern: "qc/*.png"
        ]
    }

    withName: 'GENERATE_QC_REPORT' {
        // Never cacheable, declared rather than discovered. Its inputs are collectFile()
        // results, and collectFile is an OPERATOR: it re-executes every run and writes to
        // a fresh work/tmp/<hash>/, so under Nextflow's default STANDARD hashing
        // (path + size + lastModified) the input is a new file each time. run_summary.json additionally
        // embeds a wall-clock timestamp (subworkflows/local/final_qc.nf), so its CONTENT
        // legitimately differs every run too.
        // Marking it explicitly changes no behaviour -- it already re-ran every run --
        // but it states the intent and is what makes the -resume guard assertable:
        // "every task except these two is Cached". Cheap and terminal, so nothing
        // cascades from it.
        cache = false
        publishDir = [
            path: { "${params.outdir}/qc" },
            mode: 'copy',
            saveAs: { filename -> filename.equals('versions.yml') ? null : filename }
        ]
    }

    withName: 'REGISTER' {
        cpus   = 8
        memory = {
            300.GB * task.attempt
        }
        time   = { 24.h * task.attempt }
        // POLICY: retry-exit1-then-fail. The one process whose known-transient
        // failures do NOT surface as a signal: a VALIS tile read failure and a JVM
        // OOM inside the wrapper both come back as a plain exit 1, so the cause
        // selector cannot be narrowed the way retry-then-fail narrows it. The
        // artifact is required, so the terminal branch still fails the run.
        // Pinned verbatim by tests/test_error_strategy_policy.py.
        errorStrategy = {
            task.exitStatus in [1, 104, 134, 135, 137, 139, 140, 143] ? 'retry' : 'finish'
        }

        publishDir = [
            [
                path: { "${params.outdir}/${meta.patient_id}/registered" },
                // Intermediate — see tests/test_cleanup_publish_gates.py.
                saveAs: { fn -> params.cleanup_level == 'none' ? fn : null },
                mode: 'copy',
                pattern: "registered_slides/*_registered.ome.tiff",
                overwrite: true
            ],
            [
                path: { "${params.outdir}/${meta.patient_id}/registered/summary" },
                mode: 'copy',
                pattern: "preprocessed/data/*.csv",
                overwrite: true
            ],
            [
                // The VALIS registrar pickle -- REGISTER's `registrar` emit, previously passed
                // channel-to-channel to WARP_SEG_QC only and never written to the published
                // tree. It is the whole transform for this backend (the same slot TILED_SOLVE's
                // manifest.json fills for the tiled backend, see registered/manifest below), so
                // without it a VALIS row is unscoreable by anything outside the pipeline.
                path: { "${params.outdir}/${meta.patient_id}/registered/transform" },
                mode: 'copy',
                pattern: "preprocessed/data/*_registrar.pickle",
                overwrite: true
            ]
        ]
        // maxForks: this process's cap lives in nextflow.config's "Concurrency" block,
        // AFTER `profiles {}` -- a params-derived scalar here froze before any profile ran.
    }

    // Distributed/tiled registration process configs (REG_PREP, REG_TILE, REG_NONRIGID,
    // REG_MICRO_PREP, REG_COMPOSE_*, REG_GRID, REG_WARP_TILE, REG_ASSEMBLE, COMPARE_REGISTRATION)
    // were archived on 2026-07-24 (git tag archive/tiled-valis-2026-07-24).

    // TILED fan-out processes — the ONLY STARE shape; all JVM-free and per-tile/per-slide light.
    // TILED_COARSE's global-anchor JSON is purely intermediate — TILED_SOLVE consumes it (along
    // with the tile-plan CSV) and publishes the actual manifest/TRE — so it stays unpublished
    // (see its own `enabled: false` below).
    //
    // TILED_REG_TILE's per-tile control-point JSON is NOT purely intermediate in the same way,
    // even though TILED_SOLVE also consumes it: it is the ONLY on-disk record of the per-tile
    // `error`/`ref_fg`/`mov_fg` values behind STARE's per-tile accept/reject gate, and nothing
    // downstream re-derives them once TILED_SOLVE has folded them into the manifest. An external
    // benchmark scoring that gate's ROC — a headline STARE metric no competing method reports —
    // needs this record at the scale results actually come from, not only the small in-process
    // unit-test scale, so unlike TILED_COARSE it is now published. Volume is small: a few
    // hundred bytes per tile, ~100 tiles per 20480^2 slide at the default 2048 tile size.
    withName: 'TILED_REG_TILE' {
        // Peak is the read window (tile + 2*halo), never the slide -- see the derivation above.
        // Measured 1.33 GB at the 2560^2 default window.
        memory = {
            // Inlined from the derivation above -- window = tile + 2*halo, in megapixels;
            // GB = 2 * (0.4 + 0.10/Mpx), floored at 4. A shared helper is not expressible
            // in a config file under the strict parser; see the note above the block.
            // STARE tier lookup, INLINED from lib/RegPresets.STARE. conf/*.config cannot see
            // lib/*.groovy (the name resolves silently against ConfigObject and fails only when
            // the closure runs), and the tier params are null-declared because Nextflow evaluates
            // the params block before merging CLI args. Pinned against RegPresets by
            // tests/test_reg_presets_inlined_in_config.py so the two copies cannot drift.
            // Explicit null tests, never `?:` -- Elvis treats a legitimate 0 as unset
            // (tests/test_nullable_numeric_params_no_elvis.py).
            def _p   = [high: [tile: 2048, halo: 256, out_tile: 1024],
                        medium: [tile: 1024, halo: 192, out_tile: 768],
                        low: [tile: 512, halo: 128, out_tile: 512]]
            def _row = _p[(params.reg_tiled_mode == 'custom' || !params.reg_tiled_mode) ? 'high' : params.reg_tiled_mode] ?: _p.high
            def _tile = params.reg_tiled_tile != null ? (params.reg_tiled_tile as int) : _row.tile
            def _halo = params.reg_tiled_halo != null ? (params.reg_tiled_halo as int) : _row.halo
            def win = ((_tile + 2 * _halo) as long)
            def mpx = (win * win) / 1_000_000d
            (Math.ceil(Math.max(4d, (0.4d + 0.10d * mpx) * 2d)) as int).GB * task.attempt
        }
        // maxForks: this process's cap lives in nextflow.config's "Concurrency" block,
        // AFTER `profiles {}` -- a params-derived scalar here froze before any profile ran.
        publishDir = [
            path: { "${params.outdir}/${meta.patient_id}/registered/controls" },
            mode: 'copy',
            pattern: "*_ctrl.json",
            overwrite: true
        ]
    }
    /*
        TILED_COARSE estimates M0 on a thumbnail bounded by params.reg_tiled_coarse_max_dim, read
        lazily in byte-budgeted row bands — so its peak is set by that bound, never by the slide.
        DISK+LightGlue is a U-Net, so activation memory is linear in thumbnail AREA. Measured on
        the pinned stack (torch 2.3.1 / kornia 0.7.3, CPU): 3.03 GB at 512 px, 8.78 GB at 1024 px,
        i.e. GB ≈ 1.1 + 7.3 * Mpx.

        The request is DERIVED from the resolved bound, not flat: `high` (2048 px) needs ~32 GB and
        `low` (512 px) ~3 GB, so one flat number necessarily OOMs one tier or over-reserves the
        other. Same idiom as TILED_REG_TILE and TILED_STITCH — inline the tier table, derive from
        the parameter that drives the peak.

        THE RETRY RAMP IS LINEAR IN task.attempt, DELIBERATELY. The peak here is bound-driven and
        already carries a 1.5x margin, so a retry corrects for host variance rather than searching
        for an unknown magnitude — and doubling on top of a 48 GB base would ask 384 GB by attempt 4.
    */
    withName: 'TILED_COARSE' {
        memory = {
            // STARE tier lookup, INLINED from lib/RegPresets.STARE -- conf/*.config cannot
            // see lib/*.groovy (the name resolves silently against ConfigObject and fails
            // only when the closure runs), and the tier params are null-declared because
            // Nextflow evaluates the params block before merging CLI args. Pinned against
            // RegPresets by tests/test_reg_presets_inlined_in_config.py.
            // Explicit null test, never `?:` -- Elvis treats a legitimate 0 as unset
            // (tests/test_nullable_numeric_params_no_elvis.py), and 0 means "no decimation".
            //
            // The formula degenerates below ~256 px: at `_dim` 0 (once the "no decimation"
            // escape hatch) mpx is 0 and this asks for the 4 GB floor -- the smallest request
            // in the table for the largest possible job, a guaranteed OOM now COARSE is a
            // U-Net rather than a cheap feature detector. That is not a caution any more:
            // ParamUtils.validateRegPresets THROWS at launch on any value below 256, so this
            // closure never sees one. It keeps the `Math.max(4d, ...)` floor only as the
            // arithmetic backstop it always was.
            def _p   = [high: [coarse_max_dim: 2048],
                        medium: [coarse_max_dim: 1024],
                        low: [coarse_max_dim: 512]]
            def _row = _p[(params.reg_tiled_mode == 'custom' || !params.reg_tiled_mode) ? 'high' : params.reg_tiled_mode] ?: _p.high
            def _dim = params.reg_tiled_coarse_max_dim != null ? (params.reg_tiled_coarse_max_dim as int) : _row.coarse_max_dim
            def mpx  = ((_dim as long) * (_dim as long)) / 1_000_000d
            (Math.ceil(Math.max(4d, (1.1d + 7.3d * mpx) * 1.5d)) as int).GB * task.attempt
        }
        // maxForks: this process's cap lives in nextflow.config's "Concurrency" block,
        // AFTER `profiles {}` -- a params-derived scalar here froze before any profile ran.
        // Explicit `enabled: false` rather than silence, so this is a deliberate "nothing to
        // publish here" instead of a process quietly falling through to the _UNROUTED_PUBLISH
        // guard. Unlike TILED_REG_TILE above, TILED_COARSE's global-anchor JSON carries no
        // information a downstream consumer needs once TILED_SOLVE has used it.
        publishDir = [ enabled: false ]
    }
    withName: 'TILED_SOLVE' {
        // Measured peak RSS 33 MB / 0.11 s: it reads one JSON per tile (a few hundred bytes
        // each) and writes two more. 4 GB was ~120x the observed peak; 1 GB is still ~7x the
        // Python + numpy import baseline that dominates it, and a smaller ask schedules sooner.
        memory = { 1.GB * task.attempt }   // kilobytes of control points — a tiny reduction
        // The two outlier gates on the mesh control points. `reg_tiled_max_disp` is declared
        // null in nextflow.config precisely so it can DERIVE from the read halo here: a match
        // further than the halo was never inside the read window. That is the live
        // "null means derive" contract, not a duplicated default.
        // An explicit null test, NOT `?:` -- Groovy's Elvis treats 0 as unset, and the schema
        // declares this parameter ["number", "null"] with minimum 0, so `--reg_tiled_max_disp 0`
        // (reject any non-zero displacement) would silently become 256 and reject almost
        // nothing. Guarded by tests/test_nullable_numeric_params_no_elvis.py.
        ext.args = {
            // halo is tier-owned and null-declared, so resolve it before deriving from it --
            // otherwise `--max-disp` renders as 'null'. Same inlined table as the memory closures.
            def _p   = [high: [halo: 256], medium: [halo: 192], low: [halo: 128]]
            def _row = _p[(params.reg_tiled_mode == 'custom' || !params.reg_tiled_mode) ? 'high' : params.reg_tiled_mode] ?: _p.high
            def _halo = params.reg_tiled_halo != null ? (params.reg_tiled_halo as int) : _row.halo
            def maxDisp = params.reg_tiled_max_disp != null ? params.reg_tiled_max_disp : _halo
            "--max-error ${params.reg_tiled_max_error} --max-disp ${maxDisp}"
        }
        publishDir = [
            [
                path: { "${params.outdir}/${meta.patient_id}/registered/manifest" },
                mode: 'copy',
                pattern: "*_manifest.json",
                overwrite: true
            ],
            [
                path: { "${params.outdir}/${meta.patient_id}/qc/registration" },
                mode: 'copy',
                pattern: "*_tre.json",
                overwrite: true
            ]
        ]
    }
    withName: 'TILED_STITCH' {
        // Peak is one output TILE (out_tile^2) plus the source crop it draws from -- not a row
        // strip and not the slide. Measured 1.42 GB at the 1024^2 default. See the derivation
        // above the process block.
        memory = {
            // Inlined from the derivation above -- window = out_tile, in megapixels;
            // GB = 2 * (0.6 + 0.17/Mpx), floored at 4. A shared helper is not expressible
            // in a config file under the strict parser; see the note above the block.
            // STARE tier lookup, INLINED from lib/RegPresets.STARE. conf/*.config cannot see
            // lib/*.groovy (the name resolves silently against ConfigObject and fails only when
            // the closure runs), and the tier params are null-declared because Nextflow evaluates
            // the params block before merging CLI args. Pinned against RegPresets by
            // tests/test_reg_presets_inlined_in_config.py so the two copies cannot drift.
            // Explicit null tests, never `?:` -- Elvis treats a legitimate 0 as unset
            // (tests/test_nullable_numeric_params_no_elvis.py).
            def _p   = [high: [tile: 2048, halo: 256, out_tile: 1024],
                        medium: [tile: 1024, halo: 192, out_tile: 768],
                        low: [tile: 512, halo: 128, out_tile: 512]]
            def _row = _p[(params.reg_tiled_mode == 'custom' || !params.reg_tiled_mode) ? 'high' : params.reg_tiled_mode] ?: _p.high
            def _out = params.reg_tiled_out_tile != null ? (params.reg_tiled_out_tile as int) : _row.out_tile
            def win = (_out as long)
            def mpx = (win * win) / 1_000_000d
            (Math.ceil(Math.max(4d, (0.6d + 0.17d * mpx) * 2d)) as int).GB * task.attempt
        }
        publishDir = [
            path: { "${params.outdir}/${meta.patient_id}/registered" },
            // Intermediate — see tests/test_cleanup_publish_gates.py.
            saveAs: { fn -> params.cleanup_level == 'none' ? fn : null },
            mode: 'copy',
            pattern: "registered/*_registered.ome.tiff",
            overwrite: true
        ]
        // maxForks: this process's cap lives in nextflow.config's "Concurrency" block,
        // AFTER `profiles {}` -- a params-derived scalar here froze before any profile ran.
    }

    withName: 'GENERATE_REGISTRATION_QC' {
        cpus   = 1
        memory = {
            // All THREE staged images are held at once: the reference plane, the native
            // moving plane and the registered one, plus the two-panel composite built from
            // them. An input that is read but not summed here is a silent under-request --
            // tests/test_registration_qc_wiring.py fails if any path() input is missing.
            def input_gb = ((registered.size() + native_image.size() + reference.size()) >> 30) ?: 1
            (input_gb < 20 ? 100.GB : input_gb < 50 ? 200.GB : 300.GB) * task.attempt
        }
        publishDir = [
            [
                path: { "${params.outdir}/${meta.patient_id}/qc/registration" },
                mode: 'copy',
                pattern: "qc/*_QC_RGB.{png,tif}"
            ],
            [
                path: { "${params.outdir}/${meta.patient_id}/qc/registration" },
                mode: 'copy',
                pattern: "qc/*_QC_RGB_fullres.tif"
            ]
        ]
    }

    // reg_qc=2 GeoJSON path: cell-mask label image -> cell GeoJSON.
    //
    // This block used to carry the whole StarDist vocabulary, because the process itself
    // ran StarDist: it was the one segmenter in the pipeline that ignored
    // params.seg_method. subworkflows/local/seg_qc.nf now segments with SEGMENT itself
    // (aliased SEG_QC_SEGMENT, whose block above supplies those flags for whichever
    // backend is selected), so nothing StarDist-specific belongs here any more -- and
    // neither does the GPU wiring, since contour tracing is pure CPU.
    withName: 'SEG_QC_GEOJSON' {
        // --tolerance is the ONE tunable left, and it is load-bearing: it must stay equal
        // to what EXTRACT_CELL_PROPERTIES gets, because that process traces the polygons
        // WARP_SEG_QC compares these against. bin/mask_to_geojson.py passes it straight
        // through to the same mask_to_feature_collection() the old segment_to_geojson.py
        // called, so a given mask yields byte-identical geometry across this change --
        // pinned by tests/test_seg_qc_geojson_equivalence.py.
        ext.args = { "--tolerance ${params.simplify_tolerance}" }
        // Contour tracing only: scipy.ndimage.find_objects over the label image plus one
        // find_contours per cell. Same shape and same one-mask-in-memory working set as
        // EXTRACT_CELL_PROPERTIES, so the same reservation. This is a large drop from the
        // 8 CPUs / up-to-128 GB / GPU the segmenting version needed, because the
        // segmentation moved to SEG_QC_SEGMENT and took its cost with it.
        cpus   = 1
        memory = { 64.GB * task.attempt }
        time   = { 4.h * task.attempt }
        publishDir = [
            path: { "${params.outdir}/${meta.patient_id}/qc/registration/geojson" },
            mode: 'copy',
            pattern: "*.geojson"
        ]
    }

    // reg_qc=2 GeoJSON path: warp cell polygons through each registration stage and score the
    // same, fixed cell pairs at every one.
    //
    // Exit-140 history, and why it is over. This task used to rasterize both slides' polygons
    // onto a shared whole-slide label grid (10^9-10^10 px), so peak RAM was linear in slide
    // area: 19 GB for a 60k x 40k slide, in two uint32 images. Three attempts to buy out of
    // that (16 GB/2 h, then 32 GB/6 h, then 128 GB + a --max-raster-px cap) all treated the
    // symptom; a resource ramp can only postpone a cost that scales with the input.
    //
    // The staged design removed the cause. Correspondence is fixed at the rigid stage, so IoU
    // is computed per pair inside each pair's own bounding-box window — peak RAM is ONE
    // NUCLEUS, a few thousand pixels, no matter how large the slide. Nothing in this process
    // scales with slide area any more. What remains is fixed overhead: the BioFormats JVM heap
    // (8 GB, pinned below), the valis import (~2 GB), and the flattened vertex arrays for the
    // two native GeoJSONs plus one stage (~1.5 GB at 10^6 cells x 50 vertices). 32 GB is
    // therefore generous rather than tight, and the ramp exists only for pathological inputs.
    //
    // Runtime is now the binding constraint, not memory: scoring is a Python loop over pairs
    // (~150 µs each), so 10^6 cells x 4 stages is on the order of 10 minutes.
    //
    // ONE PROCESS, TWO BACKENDS (lib/WarpBackends.groovy) since Task 3 of arch-group-c-config
    // merged WARP_SEG_QC_TILED into WARP_SEG_QC. This one withName: block is now this
    // process's sole resource owner (G4) and must size for the WORSE case — the VALIS
    // backend. The tiled backend previously ran at 8.GB/2.h and is not harmed by a larger
    // request on a cluster that allocates what it is asked for. If that becomes a
    // scheduling problem, a per-backend split must key off the `method` process INPUT
    // (e.g. by teaching lib/WarpBackends.groovy a resources() entry), NOT off
    // params.registration_method — that global is exactly what the ext.args comment a
    // few lines below explains was removed FROM THIS SAME PROCESS for reading the
    // GLOBAL PARAM while WARP_SEG_QC selects its backend from the `method` INPUT. A
    // memory/time closure keyed on the param would leak the wrong reservation onto the
    // wrong backend for the identical reason that ternary leaked `--jvm-heap-gb 8` —
    // observed once, by review, in this task. See WarpBackends' class header.
    withName: 'WARP_SEG_QC' {
        cpus   = 2
        memory = { 32.GB * (2 ** (task.attempt - 1)) }   // 32 / 64 / 128 / 256
        time   = { 3.h * task.attempt }                   // 3 / 6 / 9 / 12 h
        // TUNABLES ONLY. These two are backend-INDEPENDENT: the scorer is method-agnostic
        // (subworkflows/local/seg_qc.nf's header), so no value of the `method` input can make
        // either flag wrong, and both are things a user legitimately turns.
        //
        // `--jvm-heap-gb 8` is deliberately NOT here. It is VALIS-only and not a tunable, so it
        // resolves from lib/WarpBackends.groovy's `flags` closure, which sees the `method`
        // INPUT the process actually dispatches on. An ext.args ternary keyed on
        // params.registration_method lived here once; it read the GLOBAL PARAM while the
        // process selects its backend from an INPUT, so a caller where the two disagree could
        // leak the flag onto the JVM-free tiled backend. See WarpBackends' class header. That
        // hazard is exactly what "backend-independent" above rules out for these two.
        ext.args = { "--pairing ${params.seg_qc_pairing} --match-radius-factor ${params.seg_qc_match_radius_factor}" }
        publishDir = [
            path: { "${params.outdir}/${meta.patient_id}/qc/registration" },
            mode: 'copy',
            pattern: "*_seg_qc.json"
        ]
    }

    /*
    ================================================================================
        Reference-free cell-segmentation quality scoring (CSE, 2D) — OPT-IN
    ================================================================================
        Deliberately NOT folded into the QC errorStrategy selector above. That
        policy is retry-then-fail: it fails the run on a non-transient failure,
        which is right for an artifact whose absence leaves the output tree
        incomplete. CSE is different: it is an OPT-IN scorer whose common failure
        mode IS resource exhaustion on full-WSI masks, the memory ramp below is what
        fixes it, and its absence degrades the QC report rather than invalidating
        the run — so it retries whatever the cause, spending the ramp, and then
        drops.

        AND THE GIVING UP IS ANNOUNCED. `ignore` drops the task without failing the
        run, which is exactly how this scorer came to be described as "always
        ignored": it OOMed, the strategy swallowed it, and the run went green with
        a .zarr missing its metrics. The announcement lives in main.nf's onComplete
        handler and NOT in the errorStrategy closure — a closure in conf/*.config
        cannot log without aborting the run under the v1 config parser (see the
        POLICY comment on the closure below). The handler reports the ignored-task
        count for the whole run, so a dropped score appears in the run summary
        instead of being inferred later from an absent column.
    */
    withName: 'SEG_QUALITY_EVAL' {
        // No `label` on the process: this block sets all three of cpus/memory/time,
        // so a label would be a second owner (inert, and misleading about which one
        // wins). tests/test_resource_label_coverage.py fails on both owners.
        cpus   = 8
        memory = {
            // Peak RAM is driven by CSE's per-pixel index structures on the
            // DECOMPRESSED masks (functions.py: compute_M builds np.arange(pixels),
            // get_indices_sparse copies the mask to uint64, get_indices_pandas
            // groups every pixel) — NOT the compressed OME-TIFF size on disk. A
            // well-compressed multi-channel WSI needs far more RAM than its file
            // size suggests, so scale generously and let resourceLimits clamp.
            // First attempt is already sized for the common case so a genuine OOM
            // isn't spent climbing the retry ramp.
            def file_gb = (image.size() >> 30) ?: 1
            (file_gb < 10 ? 128.GB : file_gb < 30 ? 256.GB : 448.GB) * task.attempt
        }
        time   = { 4.h * task.attempt }
        maxRetries = 3
        // POLICY: retry-then-drop. Retry up to maxRetries whatever the cause — the
        // common failure IS resource exhaustion and the memory ramp above is what
        // fixes it — then continue the run WITHOUT this artifact. The drop is
        // deliberate: a missing CSE QualityScore degrades the QC report, it does
        // not invalidate the run. It is not a cause-selecting policy, so nothing is
        // discarded on attempt 1.
        //
        // Do NOT log from here. `log` is not bound in conf/*.config under the v1
        // config parser: it resolves against ConfigObject and throws the moment the
        // closure runs past its budget, which ABORTS the run — the exact opposite of
        // 'ignore'. Nextflow 26's v2 ConfigDsl binds it and 25.04's does not, and
        // manifest.nextflowVersion is '>=25.04.0', so this pipeline accepts at launch
        // the version it aborted on. Verified both ways 2026-08-25; see
        // tests/test_error_strategy_policy.py. The announcement moved to main.nf's
        // onComplete handler, where `log` IS bound and which counts EVERY dropped
        // task rather than only this one.
        errorStrategy = { task.attempt <= 3 ? 'retry' : 'ignore' }
        ext.when = { !params.skip_seg_quality_eval }
        // Cap scored pixels so CSE fits its memory/time budget on full-WSI masks.
        ext.args = { params.cse_max_pixels ? "--max-pixels ${params.cse_max_pixels}" : '' }
        publishDir = [
            path: { "${params.outdir}/${meta.patient_id}/qc/segmentation" },
            mode: 'copy',
            pattern: "*_seg_eval.json"
        ]
    }

    withName: 'MERGE_SEG_EVAL' {
        cpus   = 1
        memory = { 4.GB * task.attempt }
        time   = { 1.h * task.attempt }
        maxRetries = 3
        // POLICY: retry-then-drop. Dropping is acceptable here for the same reason
        // it is for SEG_QUALITY_EVAL and no other: this process only COLLATES the
        // per-patient CSE scores into a summary CSV, so losing it loses a
        // convenience view of data that is already published per patient under
        // qc/segmentation/. Nothing downstream reads it.
        //
        // Do NOT log from here — see the SEG_QUALITY_EVAL block above for why, and
        // main.nf's onComplete for where the announcement went.
        errorStrategy = { task.attempt <= 3 ? 'retry' : 'ignore' }
        ext.when = { !params.skip_seg_quality_eval }
        publishDir = [
            path: { "${params.outdir}/qc/segmentation" },
            mode: 'copy',
            pattern: "*.csv"
        ]
    }

    // NOTE: mode=add_cycle reuses EXPORT_GEOJSON / MERGE_AND_PYRAMID / MERGE_QUANT_CSVS
    // publishDir rules below. Run add_cycle with a fresh --outdir; combined outputs
    // (all cycles) are written there, leaving the prior run's outdir intact as the
    // --prior_outdir checkpoint source.
    withName: 'MERGE_AND_PYRAMID' {
        cpus   = 2
        // Streams the base resolution out of a generator of tiles and never assembles the
        // (C, H, W) stack. `_load_channel_stack` used to allocate that stack up front --
        // ~19 GB on an 8-channel 40000x30000 uint16 slide -- hand it whole to the writer,
        // and then derive each pyramid level from the previous one with BOTH resident,
        // plus downsample_image's per-channel float32 temporary, plus a slide-sized uint32
        // mask stack (8 bytes/px, on its own bigger than everything else put together).
        // That is what the old `f<20 ? 200.GB : 300.GB` ladder was chasing, and a tier
        // ladder could only ever bracket it. See bin/merge_channels_pyramid.py -- INCLUDING
        // its `maxworkers=1` pin (bin/merge_channels_pyramid.py:826-847): every write below
        // is generator-fed and zlib-compressed, and without that pin tifffile's compressor
        // pool reintroduces a term that scales with the channel count, at exactly the
        // container's pinned tifffile version (2025.5.10), which this closure was NOT
        // re-derived against -- it predates that pin and was calibrated on a 2023.4.12 host.
        //
        // Four terms now, and only one of them carries the channel count:
        //  * ONE float32 base plane plus the transients around it -- 3.25 uint16 planes,
        //    MEASURED rather than derived: at 4096^2 with a one-tile-row band and 8 levels
        //    the peak is 3.510 / 3.546 / 3.879 / 4.546 planes at C=1/4/8/16, whose slope is
        //    the stash term below and whose intercept, fit over C=4/8/16 (exact there --
        //    C=1 sits 0.21 planes ABOVE the fit, a finite-C edge effect at the smallest
        //    channel count that the +1d adder below absorbs), is 3.21. It comes from
        //    `_downsample_plane_f32` transiently holding the float32 plane (2 uint16 planes)
        //    plus `resized` (0.5), `np.round`'s output (0.5) and `.astype`'s (0.25) -- 2 +
        //    0.5 + 0.5 + 0.25 = 3.25, taken as the coefficient rather than the measured 3.21
        //    so this term does not undershoot the mechanism's own arithmetic (the earlier
        //    `3.11` here undershot both the measured intercept and the mechanism's sum).
        //    This term is irreducible:
        //    cv2.INTER_AREA's effective ratio is `h / (h // factor)`, so a pyramid level
        //    cannot be assembled from bands (measured, 29642 counts of error on an odd
        //    height) and has to come from a whole plane.
        //  * levels 2 and deeper for EVERY channel, held because tifffile fills the
        //    reserved SubIFDs one level at a time while level k is derived from level k-1.
        //    Geometric in the scale factor, which is why `pyramid_scale` and
        //    `pyramid_resolutions` are real derivation sources and not decoration: at
        //    `--pyramid-resolutions 2` there is no stash at all, and at scale 4 it is a
        //    sixteenth of what it is at scale 2.
        //  * one read band (READ_BAND_BYTES, 64 MiB) and the write-tile buffers.
        //  * the mask series, which is STREAMED now: one uint32 band, not (2, H, W).
        //
        // A decoded plane's size is not knowable from a config closure, so it is taken as
        // 4x the LARGEST single channel file. Deliberately the largest file and not the sum
        // of them -- the sum is the O(C) shape this rewrite removed.
        //
        // 4:1 IS A FLOOR ESTIMATE, NOT A BOUND. Measured on 2048^2 uint16 planes at
        // SPLIT_CHANNELS' own zlib-tiled settings:
        //     uniform noise                                 1.0x
        //     dim Poisson + 10% signal                       1.9x
        //     smooth, low-entropy                            3.6x
        //     75% true-black background + Poisson signal     4.2x
        //     near-empty / failed channel                  796x
        // A WSI is mostly empty glass and every channel shares that background, so
        // `sizes.max()` does not rescue the estimate -- all the channels compress well
        // together. The request scales as 4/r, so at r=6 a 60000x60000 C=10 slide asks
        // ~18 GB against a ~25 GB peak, and at r=30 a 100000x80000 C=12 slide asks ~9 GB
        // against ~57 GB.
        //
        // What actually backstops that is conf/base.config's errorStrategy: exit 137
        // (SIGKILL, the OOM code) is a retry, `maxRetries = 3`, and every term here is
        // multiplied by `task.attempt` -- so attempts 1..4 cover a shortfall of up to 4x.
        // Beyond that the task fails loudly rather than silently under-running. The 100000
        // x80000 case above is NOT covered, and is recorded here so the next reader can see
        // where the estimate breaks rather than rediscovering it from a SIGKILL.
        //
        // IF A PRODUCTION channels/ DIRECTORY IS AVAILABLE, MEASURE r AND RAISE THE
        // COEFFICIENT. `r = (H * W * 2) / file_size` per channel, taken over real slides;
        // the 4 here is a guess made without one, chosen low because the retry ramp is the
        // safety net and a 300 GB reservation was the problem being fixed.
        memory = {
            // ONE-FILE GROUPS. Nextflow binds a `path` input to a bare Path when the
            // group holds exactly one file, and to a List when it holds more. On the
            // bare Path, `.collect{}` iterates the path's NAME ELEMENTS -- so
            // `it.size()` went looking for a file literally called "channels" (the
            // stageAs directory) and the run ABORTED with
            // "No such file or directory: channels".
            //
            // A one-channel patient is an ordinary input: quantify_markers.nf groups
            // one-tiff-per-marker entries per patient, so a single-marker panel makes
            // a group of one. Every nf-test in this module passed 2 or 3 files, which
            // is why the suite was green -- tests/modules/merge_and_pyramid.nf.test
            // now carries the one-file case.
            //
            // Normalised here rather than shared: conf/*.config cannot define a helper
            // under NF26's strict parser, and cannot see lib/*.groovy (see CLAUDE.md).
            def files    = (split_channels instanceof Collection) ? split_channels : [split_channels]
            def sizes    = files.collect { it.size() }
            def largest  = sizes ? sizes.max() : 0L
            def plane    = (largest * 4d) / (1024d * 1024d * 1024d)
            def scale    = (params.pyramid_scale as double)
            def ratio    = 1d / (scale * scale)
            // Sum of the stashed levels as a geometric series taken to its limit: an upper
            // bound on the finite sum, tight to within `ratio ** (levels - 2)`.
            def stash    = (params.pyramid_resolutions as int) > 2 ?
                           sizes.size() * plane * ratio * ratio / (1d - ratio) : 0d
            (Math.ceil(Math.max(8d, plane * 3.25d + stash + 1d)) as int).GB * task.attempt
        }
        time   = { 8.h * task.attempt }

        publishDir = [
            path: { "${params.outdir}/${meta.patient_id}/pyramid" },
            mode: 'copy',
            saveAs: { filename -> filename.equals('versions.yml') ? null : filename }
        ]
    }

    // ALSO MATCHES `SEG_QC_SEGMENT`, the reg_qc=2 QC's aliased include of this same process
    // (subworkflows/local/seg_qc.nf). Nextflow matches a config selector against an alias'
    // OWN name and its process' original declared name -- the SPLIT_CHANNELS /
    // SPLIT_PRIOR_PYRAMID block below documents that, confirmed from a real run's debug
    // log. Everything here is meant to be shared with the alias: same process body, same
    // backend table, same per-image resource shape. publishDir is NOT, and the
    // `withName: 'SEG_QC_SEGMENT'` block below overrides it for exactly that reason.
    withName: 'SEGMENT' {
        // Per-backend TOOL arguments. SEGMENT had no ext.args entry at all, so its
        // `task.ext.args ?: ''` was permanently empty and every tunable was hardcoded
        // in the process script -- the opposite of CLAUDE.md's "tool arguments go in
        // conf/modules.config via ext.args". They live here now; the backend's identity
        // (container, entry point, versions rows) and its RESOLVED flags (output prefix,
        // nuclear channel index) stay in lib/SegBackends.groovy, because those are not
        // things an operator tunes.
        //
        // Keyed on params.seg_method, like the container directive and the backend
        // table. An unknown value throws in SegBackends.of() before this is reached.
        ext.args = {
            def flags = [
                // The StarDist vocabulary (model, tiling, expansion, normalization/
                // detection thresholds), INLINED rather than shared from a helper: a
                // config file cannot declare one under the strict parser, and neither a
                // top-level closure binding nor a lib/*.groovy class is a legal
                // substitute -- see the long note at the top of this file. This is its
                // only call site, and the reg_qc=2 QC segments through this same process
                // (aliased SEG_QC_SEGMENT), so there is nothing left for it to drift
                // against. tests/modules/segment.nf.test pins the rendered flag string.
                stardist  : [
                    // --model-dir only when a custom StarDist model directory is set; otherwise
                    // the script loads --model-name as a built-in pretrained model (e.g.
                    // 2D_versatile_fluo). The shipped segmentation_model name is NOT a StarDist
                    // built-in (nextflow.config:117) -- without --model-dir, segment.py raises
                    // FileNotFoundError trying to load it, i.e. `--seg_method stardist` runs
                    // unshipped. That used to sink the reg_qc=2 QC too, silently: SEG_QC_GEOJSON ran
                    // StarDist regardless of seg_method and its failure is swallowed by the QC
                    // errorStrategy's 'ignore' branch. The QC follows seg_method now, so at the
                    // shipped default (instantseg) it no longer touches this flag at all.
                    params.segmentation_model_dir ? "--model-dir ${params.segmentation_model_dir}" : '',
                    "--model-name ${params.segmentation_model}",
                    "--n-tiles ${params.seg_n_tiles_y} ${params.seg_n_tiles_x}",
                    "--expand-distance ${params.seg_expand_distance}",
                    "--pmin ${params.seg_pmin}",
                    "--pmax ${params.seg_pmax}",
                    // Detection probability threshold (lower = detect fainter nuclei).
                    // null -> use the model's built-in default.
                    params.seg_prob_thresh != null ? "--prob-thresh ${params.seg_prob_thresh}" : '',
                ],
                instantseg: [
                    "--model-name ${params.seg_instantseg_model}",
                    "--target ${params.seg_instantseg_target}",
                    // meta.pixel_size, not params.pixel_size: bin/segment_instantseg.py
                    // declares `type=float` for --pixel-size, so the literal string
                    // 'auto' (the shipped params.pixel_size default) fails argparse
                    // outright. meta.pixel_size is the number INPUT_CHECK's
                    // PREFLIGHT_SCALE already resolved per-slide (conf/*.config cannot
                    // reference lib/*.groovy classes, but `meta` IS in scope here since
                    // SEGMENT's input tuple carries it).
                    "--pixel-size ${meta.pixel_size}",
                    "--tile-size ${params.seg_instantseg_tile_size}",
                    "--batch-size ${params.seg_instantseg_batch_size}",
                ],
                cellsam   : [
                    "--expand-distance ${params.seg_expand_distance}",
                    "--bbox-threshold ${params.seg_cellsam_bbox_threshold}",
                    "--block-size ${params.seg_cellsam_block_size}",
                    "--overlap ${params.seg_cellsam_overlap}",
                    // use_wsi toggles CellSAM's native tiling; model_path points at
                    // pre-downloaded weights (omit -> auto-download, needs a token).
                    params.seg_cellsam_use_wsi ? '--use-wsi' : '',
                    params.cellsam_model_path ? "--model-path ${params.cellsam_model_path}" : '',
                ],
            ]
            // All three entry points take --use-gpu, so it is shared rather than repeated.
            ((flags[params.seg_method] ?: []) + [params.seg_gpu ? '--use-gpu' : ''])
                .findAll { it }.join(' ')
        }
        // 8 CPUs so a CPU path (or CPU-bound pre/post steps like label expansion
        // and Dask tiling) is tolerable; GPU inference is unaffected by this.
        cpus   = 8
        memory = {
            def file_gb = (merged_file.size() >> 30) ?: 1
            (file_gb < 10 ? 32.GB : file_gb < 30 ? 64.GB : 128.GB) * task.attempt
        }
        time   = { 4.h * task.attempt }
        // APPEND, do not replace. A withName: clusterOptions assignment OVERRIDES
        // the `slurm` profile's process.clusterOptions outright -- so SEGMENT, the
        // one process that must carry --gres, was also the one process that lost
        // the site's --account/--qos, and it is the process a scheduler is most
        // likely to reject without them.
        //
        // The account/qos derivation is INLINED from nextflow.config's `slurm`
        // profile rather than composed from it. Two things rule composition out:
        //
        //   * `task.clusterOptions` inside a clusterOptions closure is
        //     self-referential. Verified 2026-08-25 on NXF_VER=26.04.6: it does
        //     not fall back to the profile value, it recurses --
        //     java.lang.StackOverflowError, task failed, run aborted.
        //   * conf/*.config cannot see lib/*.groovy and NF26's parser forbids a
        //     shared helper between blocks, so there is nowhere else to put it.
        //
        // Same forced duplication as the inlined RegPresets tables above.
        // tests/test_cluster_options_compose.py asserts the two derivations keep
        // reading the same params, which is the part that would otherwise drift.
        clusterOptions   = {
            def opts = []
            if (params.slurm_account && params.slurm_account != '') opts << "--account=${params.slurm_account}"
            if (params.slurm_qos && params.slurm_qos != '')         opts << "--qos=${params.slurm_qos}"
            if (params.seg_gpu)                                     opts << "--gres=gpu:${params.gpu_type}"
            return opts ? opts.join(' ') : null
        }
        // Singularity needs --nv to bind the host NVIDIA driver stack into the
        // container; without it torch.cuda.is_available() is False even when
        // SLURM has granted the GPU via --gres.
        //
        // For the CellSAM backend, also forward DEEPCELL_ACCESS_TOKEN from the
        // launch environment so cellsam_pipeline can auto-download weights when
        // --cellsam_model_path is unset. Both engines forward by NAME only --
        // the value itself is never interpolated into containerOptions, so it
        // never lands in the work dir's .command.run (group-readable on a
        // typical shared HPC filesystem, and retained across -resume).
        // Docker: '-e VAR' reads the value from the launching shell's
        // environment at run time. Singularity: singularity.envWhitelist
        // (nextflow.config, 'singularity' profile) does the equivalent --
        // contributes nothing here. Only added when the backend is CellSAM
        // and the token is actually set.
        containerOptions = {
            def opts = []
            // Docker and Singularity bind GPUs with different flags. --nv is
            // Singularity-only; Docker needs --gpus all, else the GPU is never
            // mounted and CellSAM silently falls back to CPU (4+ day WSI runs).
            if (params.seg_gpu) opts << (workflow.containerEngine == 'docker' ? '--gpus all' : '--nv')
            if (params.seg_method == 'cellsam' && workflow.containerEngine == 'docker' && System.getenv('DEEPCELL_ACCESS_TOKEN')) {
                opts << '-e DEEPCELL_ACCESS_TOKEN'
            }
            opts.join(' ')
        }
        // Publish masks where the postprocessing checkpoint CSV records them
        // (postprocess.nf builds ${outdir}/<patient_id>/segmentation/<mask>).
        // Without this explicit rule the default selector would publish to
        // ${outdir}/segment/, so the checkpoint path would point at files that
        // are not there (breaking --start postprocessing resume and lookups).
        publishDir = [
            path: { "${params.outdir}/${meta.patient_id}/segmentation" },
            // Intermediate — see tests/test_cleanup_publish_gates.py.
            mode: 'copy',
            saveAs: { fn -> (params.cleanup_level == 'none' && !fn.equals('versions.yml')) ? fn : null }
        ]
    }

    // The reg_qc=2 QC segmentation -- `SEGMENT as SEG_QC_SEGMENT`, see
    // subworkflows/local/seg_qc.nf. SEGMENT's block above already applies to it (alias
    // matching, see SPLIT_PRIOR_PYRAMID below) and is meant to: container, resources,
    // backend ext.args and the GPU wiring are all shared. This block overrides the two
    // things that must NOT be. Like SPLIT_PRIOR_PYRAMID's, it MUST stay textually after
    // the block it overrides -- Nextflow applies later matching selectors on top of
    // earlier ones, per field.
    withName: 'SEG_QC_SEGMENT' {
        // These masks are NOT published. They are per-SLIDE intermediates traced from the
        // NATIVE images and consumed within the run by SEG_QC_GEOJSON, whereas
        // <pid>/segmentation/ is indexed by csv/segmented.csv, which names exactly one
        // per-PATIENT mask cut from the merged registered reference. Inheriting SEGMENT's
        // publishDir would drop unindexed files into a manifest-owned tree -- and, on the
        // stardist backend, under names derived from the input image stem, so two slides
        // of one patient would not even collide loudly. The published QC artifact is the
        // GeoJSON (SEG_QC_GEOJSON's own publishDir), which is what a human reads.
        publishDir = [ enabled: false ]
        // Per-SLIDE, not per-patient. A patient contributes several native slides here and
        // SEGMENT names its size log `${prefix}.SEGMENT.size.csv`, so the default prefix
        // (meta.patient_id) would give every slide of a patient the SAME log filename --
        // and AGGREGATE_SIZE_LOGS stages them all into one directory. The instantseg and
        // cellsam entry points take `--prefix` too, so their masks are disambiguated by
        // the same value. meta.qc_slide is set in subworkflows/local/seg_qc.nf.
        ext.prefix = { "${meta.qc_slide}" }
    }

    withName: 'SPLIT_CHANNELS' {
        cpus   = 1
        memory = {
            def file_gb = (registered_image.size() >> 30) ?: 1
            (file_gb < 5 ? 32.GB : file_gb < 15 ? 64.GB : 128.GB) * task.attempt
        }
        time   = { 2.h * task.attempt }
        // Per-patient, because bin/split_multichannel.py names its outputs by sanitised
        // channel name alone ('PANCK.tiff'). Under the run-level default this process
        // inherited, two patients sharing a marker published to the same path and the
        // second silently overwrote the first — observed on a two-patient run.
        publishDir = [
            path: { "${params.outdir}/${meta.patient_id}/split_channels" },
            // Intermediate — see tests/test_cleanup_publish_gates.py.
            mode: 'copy',
            saveAs: { fn -> (params.cleanup_level == 'none' && !fn.equals('versions.yml')) ? fn : null }
        ]
    }

    // `subworkflows/local/add_cycle.nf` includes SPLIT_CHANNELS a second time, aliased
    // `SPLIT_PRIOR_PYRAMID` (re-splitting the prior run's pyramid so its channels can be
    // deduped against the new cycle's — see add_cycle.nf's ch_prior_tagged/ch_new_tagged
    // priority merge). Nextflow's config selector matches an alias against BOTH its own
    // name and the process' ORIGINAL declared name (`withName: 'SPLIT_CHANNELS'` above
    // matches SPLIT_PRIOR_PYRAMID tasks too, confirmed via `Config settings
    // withName:SPLIT_CHANNELS matches process SPLIT_PRIOR_PYRAMID` in a real run's debug
    // log) — so WITHOUT this block, both instances published `*.tiff` by bare channel
    // name into the identical `<pid>/split_channels/` directory, and whichever task
    // finished last silently overwrote the other's file for any marker name the two
    // shared. This block wins for SPLIT_PRIOR_PYRAMID specifically because Nextflow
    // applies later-defined matching selectors on top of earlier ones (per-field, not
    // whole-block) — it MUST stay textually after the `withName: 'SPLIT_CHANNELS'`
    // block above, or SPLIT_CHANNELS' publishDir would win back for both. cpus/memory/
    // time are intentionally left unset here: SPLIT_CHANNELS' block above still owns
    // them for both aliases (unaffected, since this block never sets those fields).
    withName: 'SPLIT_PRIOR_PYRAMID' {
        publishDir = [
            path: { "${params.outdir}/${meta.patient_id}/split_channels/prior" },
            // Intermediate — see tests/test_cleanup_publish_gates.py.
            mode: 'copy',
            saveAs: { fn -> (params.cleanup_level == 'none' && !fn.equals('versions.yml')) ? fn : null }
        ]
    }

    withName: 'EXTRACT_CELL_PROPERTIES' {
        cpus   = 1
        memory = { 64.GB * task.attempt }
        time   = { 12.h * task.attempt }
        // Slimmer GeoJSON defaults (smaller file = faster QuPath import). The exported
        // polygon is display-only — all quantification is mask-derived — so simplifying
        // it and dropping sub-pixel coordinate precision does not affect analysis.
        ext.args = { "--simplify_tolerance ${params.simplify_tolerance} --coord_precision ${params.geojson_coord_precision}" }
        publishDir = [
            path: { "${params.outdir}/${meta.patient_id}/cell_properties" },
            // Intermediate — see tests/test_cleanup_publish_gates.py.
            mode: 'copy',
            saveAs: { fn -> (params.cleanup_level == 'none' && !fn.equals('versions.yml')) ? fn : null }
        ]
    }

    withName: 'EXTRACT_NUCLEI_PROPERTIES' {
        cpus   = 1
        memory = { 64.GB * task.attempt }
        time   = { 12.h * task.attempt }
        // Same slimmer contour settings for the nucleus polygons.
        ext.args = { "--simplify_tolerance ${params.simplify_tolerance} --coord_precision ${params.geojson_coord_precision}" }
        // path stops at 'cell_properties' (not '.../cell_properties/nuclei'): the
        // process now writes its outputs into a nuclei/ subdirectory of its own task
        // dir (see modules/local/extract_nuclei_properties.nf), so Nextflow's default
        // publishDir behaviour preserves that relative subdirectory on copy, exactly
        // as REGISTER's registered_slides/ and EXPORT_GEOJSON's export/ do. This keeps
        // the published location identical (.../cell_properties/nuclei/*) while making
        // it derivable via Layout.publishedPath(outdir, pid, 'cell_properties', file)
        // instead of a config-only path segment Layout has no way to see.
        publishDir = [
            path: { "${params.outdir}/${meta.patient_id}/cell_properties" },
            // Intermediate — see tests/test_cleanup_publish_gates.py.
            mode: 'copy',
            saveAs: { fn -> (params.cleanup_level == 'none' && !fn.equals('versions.yml')) ? fn : null }
        ]
    }

    withName: 'QUANTIFY' {
        cpus   = 1
        memory = { 128.GB * task.attempt }
        time   = { 12.h * task.attempt }
        // Median is always computed (the default statistic). --expanded adds Mean +
        // Sum per compartment on top; independent of quantify_compartments (which
        // only controls whether the nuclear mask is routed in for Nucleus/Cytoplasm).
        ext.args = { params.expanded_quantification ? '--expanded' : '' }
        // Per-patient for the same reason as SPLIT_CHANNELS. QUANTIFY's filenames do
        // carry a patient prefix ('P001_CD3_quant.csv'), so this is not fixing a live
        // collision — it is removing the last two processes that depend on the
        // fall-through default, so that default can become a guard.
        publishDir = [
            path: { "${params.outdir}/${meta.patient_id}/quantify" },
            // Intermediate — see tests/test_cleanup_publish_gates.py.
            mode: 'copy',
            saveAs: { fn -> (params.cleanup_level == 'none' && !fn.equals('versions.yml')) ? fn : null }
        ]
    }

    withName: 'EXPORT_GEOJSON' {
        cpus   = 1
        memory = { 32.GB * task.attempt }
        time   = { 2.h * task.attempt }
        // `meta.pixel_size`, not `params.pixel_size`: see modules/local/export_geojson.nf's
        // script: comment. This renders a SECOND `--pixel_size` after the script's own
        // explicit flag, and argparse's last-wins semantics means THIS is the value that
        // actually reaches the tool -- so it must carry the resolved number too, or the
        // script's fix is silently overridden back to the literal 'auto'.
        ext.args = { "--pixel_size ${meta.pixel_size}" }
        publishDir = [
            path: { "${params.outdir}/${meta.patient_id}/geojson" },
            mode: 'copy',
            overwrite: true
        ]
    }

    withName: 'EXPORT_SPATIALDATA' {
        // The .zarr is a directory and the process already emits it under
        // `spatialdata/`, so publish at the patient root — otherwise the path
        // double-nests to <patient>/spatialdata/spatialdata/<id>.zarr.
        publishDir = [
            path: { "${params.outdir}/${meta.patient_id}" },
            mode: 'copy',
            pattern: "spatialdata/**"
        ]
        time   = { 4.h * task.attempt }
    }

    /*
    ================================================================================
        Pixie Clustering Processes
    ================================================================================
    */

    withName: 'MERGE_QUANT_CSVS' {
        publishDir = [
            path: { "${params.outdir}/${meta.patient_id}/quantification" },
            mode: 'copy',
            saveAs: { filename -> filename.equals('versions.yml') ? null : filename }
        ]
    }

    /*
        NOTE: this is the one publishDir override in this file WITHOUT the
        `saveAs: { filename -> filename.equals('versions.yml') ? null : filename }`
        clause that the top-of-file `_UNROUTED_PUBLISH` default block and every other
        override carry, so
        `<outdir>/size_logs/versions.yml` is genuinely published. Its only content is
        the emitting process's fully-qualified name, which means this block is what
        makes AGGREGATE_SIZE_LOGS' position in the workflow graph USER-VISIBLE: when
        the call moved into subworkflows/local/final_qc.nf, the published key changed
        from "MIRAGE:AGGREGATE_SIZE_LOGS" to "MIRAGE:FINAL_QC:AGGREGATE_SIZE_LOGS".
        Left as-is deliberately: adding the saveAs would align this block with the
        convention but would DELETE a file that ships today, and removing a published
        artifact is a bigger behaviour change than the diagnostic string inside it.
    */
    withName: 'AGGREGATE_SIZE_LOGS' {
        // Never cacheable, declared rather than discovered. Its inputs are collectFile()
        // results, and collectFile is an OPERATOR: it re-executes every run and writes to
        // a fresh work/tmp/<hash>/, so under Nextflow's default STANDARD hashing
        // (path + size + lastModified) the input is a new file each time.
        // Marking it explicitly changes no behaviour -- it already re-ran every run --
        // but it states the intent and is what makes the -resume guard assertable:
        // "every task except these two is Cached". Cheap and terminal, so nothing
        // cascades from it.
        cache = false
        publishDir = [
            path: { "${params.outdir}/size_logs" },
            mode: 'copy'
        ]
    }

}
